  {
   "key": "overview",
   "title": "Quick Overview",
   "position": 0,
   "status": "ready",
   "kind": "backbone",
   "target_minutes": 3.0,
   "content": {
    "summary": "Requests is an elegant and human-centric HTTP client library for Python built on top of urllib3 with a modular internal design.",
    "blocks": [
     {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Quick Overview",
      "level": 2
     },
     {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Requests is an HTTP library written in Python designed specifically for human beings [E2]. The project aims to make interacting with web services simple and intuitive, removing the boilerplates normally associated with low-level network operations [E19]. As an HTTP client, it abstracts away complex tasks like connection pooling, payload encoding, and cookie persistence."
     },
     {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Library Design and Structure",
      "level": 3
     },
     {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "From version 1.0.0 onwards, Requests adopted a modular internal design [E10]. This architecture introduces features such as Transport Adapters, which allow developers to define custom interaction methods and apply per-service configuration [E10]. By default, Requests uses urllib3 to handle the heavy lifting of network transport, connection pooling, and secure socket management [E6, E10]."
     },
     {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "import requests\n\nr = requests.get('https://www.python.org')\nprint(r.status_code)",
      "path": "src/requests/__init__.py",
      "start_line": 10,
      "caption": "Basic GET usage in Requests"
     },
     {
      "type": "qa",
      "question": "What underlying library does Requests use for HTTP operations?",
      "answer": "Requests relies on urllib3 for its core HTTP and HTTPS interactions, providing underlying connection pooling and performance optimizations.",
      "kind": "repository"
     },
     {
      "type": "diagram",
      "diagram_id": "dg:1cea2a4bdb009245",
      "caption": "requests architecture"
     }
    ]
   },
